# Line endings and binary safety.
#
# Ported from Tusk's Vault after a real failure there: twelve bundled web fonts
# were committed without a `binary` rule, eol conversion rewrote their bytes,
# and every one of them was ~80% larger than the length in its own header. The
# browser rejected all twelve, the site silently fell back to a system font,
# and none of it was visible on a machine that happened to have the fonts
# installed locally. The bug survived because its symptom appears nowhere near
# its cause.
#
# This repository ships no binaries today. The rule is here anyway, because a
# Foundry module acquires a cover image or an icon the moment it gets a listing
# page, and the failure above is cheaper to prevent than to notice.

# `binary` is shorthand for `-text -diff`: no eol conversion on any machine,
# whatever the contributor's core.autocrlf happens to be.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.webp  binary
*.ico   binary
*.svg   text
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.webm  binary
*.mp4   binary
*.ogg   binary

# The module's own sources. Foundry reads these from a zip on every platform,
# and LF everywhere keeps the built artefact byte-identical no matter which
# machine cut the release.
*.js    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.css   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf

# The POSIX side: a CR reaching a shebang produces
# "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M".
*.sh    text eol=lf
